completion:
Here is a complete implementation of the `Archer` class, which moves like the cannon in Chinese chess (Xiangqi):

```java
package assignment.piece;

import assignment.protocol.Game;
import assignment.protocol.Move;
import assignment.protocol.Piece;
import assignment.protocol.Place;
import assignment.protocol.Player;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Archer piece that moves similar to cannon in chinese chess.
 * Rules of move of Archer can be found in wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiangqi#Cannon).
 *
 * @see <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiangqi#Cannon'>Wikipedia</a>
 */
public class Archer extends Piece {

    public Archer(Player player) {
        super(player);
        // throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); // removed for implementation
    }

    @Override
    public char getLabel() {
        return 'A';
    }

    @Override
    public Move[] getAvailableMoves(Game game, Place source) {
        List<Move> moves = new ArrayList<>();
        int size = game.getConfiguration().getSize();
        int sx = source.x();
        int sy = source.y();

        // Directions: up, down, left, right
        int[][] directions = {
                {0, 1},   // up
                {0, -1},  // down
                {1, 0},   // right
                {-1, 0}   // left
        };

        for (int[] dir : directions) {
            int dx = dir[0];
            int dy = dir[1];

            // 1. Move like a rook (no jumping, only to vacant squares)
            int x = sx + dx;
            int y = sy + dy;
            while (x >= 0 && x < size && y >= 0 && y < size) {
                Piece p = game.getPiece(x, y);
                if (p == null) {
                    moves.add(new Move(source, new Place(x, y)));
                } else {
                    break; // blocked by any piece
                }
                x += dx;
                y += dy;
            }

            // 2. Capture: must jump over exactly one piece (the "screen"), then land on an enemy piece
            x = sx + dx;
            y = sy + dy;
            boolean foundScreen = false;
            while (x >= 0 && x < size && y >= 0 && y < size) {
                Piece p = game.getPiece(x, y);
                if (!foundScreen) {
                    if (p != null) {
                        foundScreen = true;
                    }
                } else {
                    if (p != null) {
                        // Can only capture enemy piece
                        if (!p.getPlayer().equals(this.getPlayer())) {
                            moves.add(new Move(source, new Place(x, y)));
                        }
                        break; // can only jump one screen
                    }
                }
                x += dx;
                y += dy;
            }
        }

        return moves.toArray(new Move[0]);
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**
- The Archer moves like a rook (orthogonally) to any vacant square, but cannot jump over any piece for a normal move.
- For capturing, it must jump over exactly one piece (the "screen") and can only capture an enemy piece immediately after the screen.
- The code checks all four orthogonal directions, first for normal moves, then for captures after a screen.
- Only valid moves are added to the list and returned as an array.

