completion:
Here is a **complete implementation** of the `Archer` class, which models the "Cannon" (炮) from Xiangqi (Chinese Chess):

```java
package assignment.piece;

import assignment.protocol.Game;
import assignment.protocol.Move;
import assignment.protocol.Piece;
import assignment.protocol.Place;
import assignment.protocol.Player;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Archer piece that moves similar to cannon in chinese chess.
 * Rules of move of Archer can be found in wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiangqi#Cannon).
 *
 * @see <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiangqi#Cannon'>Wikipedia</a>
 */
public class Archer extends Piece {
    public Archer(Player player) {
        super(player);
        // throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); // removed for implementation
    }

    @Override
    public char getLabel() {
        return 'A';
    }

    /**
     * Returns an array of moves that are valid given the current place of the piece.
     * Archer moves like a rook for non-capturing moves, but for capturing must jump over exactly one piece.
     */
    @Override
    public Move[] getAvailableMoves(Game game, Place source) {
        List<Move> moves = new ArrayList<>();
        int size = game.getConfiguration().getSize();
        int[][] directions = {
                {1, 0},   // right
                {-1, 0},  // left
                {0, 1},   // down
                {0, -1}   // up
        };

        for (int[] dir : directions) {
            int x = source.x();
            int y = source.y();

            // 1. Non-capturing moves: like rook, until blocked
            int nx = x + dir[0];
            int ny = y + dir[1];
            while (nx >= 0 && nx < size && ny >= 0 && ny < size) {
                Piece p = game.getPiece(nx, ny);
                if (p == null) {
                    moves.add(new Move(source, new Place(nx, ny)));
                } else {
                    break; // blocked by any piece
                }
                nx += dir[0];
                ny += dir[1];
            }

            // 2. Capturing moves: must jump over exactly one piece (the "screen"), then land on an enemy piece
            nx = x + dir[0];
            ny = y + dir[1];
            boolean screenFound = false;
            while (nx >= 0 && nx < size && ny >= 0 && ny < size) {
                Piece p = game.getPiece(nx, ny);
                if (!screenFound) {
                    if (p != null) {
                        screenFound = true; // found the screen
                    }
                } else {
                    if (p != null) {
                        // Can only capture if it's an enemy piece
                        if (!p.getPlayer().equals(this.getPlayer())) {
                            moves.add(new Move(source, new Place(nx, ny)));
                        }
                        break; // can only jump over one screen
                    }
                }
                nx += dir[0];
                ny += dir[1];
            }
        }
        return moves.toArray(new Move[0]);
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**
- **Non-capturing moves:** Like a rook, move in straight lines until blocked.
- **Capturing moves:** Must jump over exactly one piece (the "screen"), then can capture the first enemy piece after the screen in the same direction.
- **No move can go off the board.**
- **No capturing own pieces.**

This implementation should pass all reasonable tests for the Archer/Cannon movement in Xiangqi.

