Here is a complete implementation of the `Archer` class, which behaves like the "Cannon" in Xiangqi (Chinese Chess):

```java
package assignment.piece;

import assignment.protocol.Game;
import assignment.protocol.Move;
import assignment.protocol.Piece;
import assignment.protocol.Place;
import assignment.protocol.Player;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Archer piece that moves similar to cannon in chinese chess.
 * Rules of move of Archer can be found in wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiangqi#Cannon).
 *
 * @see <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiangqi#Cannon'>Wikipedia</a>
 */
public class Archer extends Piece {
    public Archer(Player player) {
        super(player);
        // throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); // removed for implementation
    }

    @Override
    public char getLabel() {
        return 'A';
    }

    @Override
    public Move[] getAvailableMoves(Game game, Place source) {
        List<Move> moves = new ArrayList<>();
        int size = game.getConfiguration().getSize();
        int[] dx = {1, -1, 0, 0}; // right, left, down, up
        int[] dy = {0, 0, 1, -1};

        for (int dir = 0; dir < 4; dir++) {
            int x = source.x();
            int y = source.y();

            // 1. Move like a rook (without jumping over any piece)
            while (true) {
                x += dx[dir];
                y += dy[dir];
                if (x < 0 || x >= size || y < 0 || y >= size) break;
                Piece p = game.getPiece(x, y);
                if (p == null) {
                    moves.add(new Move(source, new Place(x, y)));
                } else {
                    break; // blocked by a piece
                }
            }

            // 2. Capture by jumping over exactly one piece (the "screen")
            x = source.x();
            y = source.y();
            boolean foundScreen = false;
            while (true) {
                x += dx[dir];
                y += dy[dir];
                if (x < 0 || x >= size || y < 0 || y >= size) break;
                Piece p = game.getPiece(x, y);
                if (!foundScreen) {
                    if (p != null) {
                        foundScreen = true;
                    }
                } else {
                    if (p != null) {
                        // Can capture if it's an enemy piece
                        if (!p.getPlayer().equals(this.getPlayer())) {
                            moves.add(new Move(source, new Place(x, y)));
                        }
                        break; // can only capture the first piece after the screen
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return moves.toArray(new Move[0]);
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**
- The Archer moves like a rook (orthogonally) any number of vacant squares.
- To capture, it must jump over exactly one piece (the "screen") and land on an enemy piece.
- The code first collects all non-capturing moves (like a rook), then looks for a "screen" and, after that, the first enemy piece to capture.
- The `getAvailableMoves` method returns all valid moves for the Archer from the given position.

