```java
package assignment.piece;

import assignment.protocol.Game;
import assignment.protocol.Move;
import assignment.protocol.Piece;
import assignment.protocol.Place;
import assignment.protocol.Player;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Knight piece that moves similar to knight in chess.
 * Rules of move of Knight can be found in wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knight_(chess)).
 *
 * @see <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knight_(chess)'>Wikipedia</a>
 */
public class Knight extends Piece {
    public Knight(Player player) {
        super(player);
    }

    @Override
    public char getLabel() {
        return 'K';
    }

    /**
     * Returns an array of moves that are valid given the current place of the piece.
     * Given the {@link Game} object and the {@link Place} that current knight piece locates, this method should
     * return ALL VALID {@link Move}s according to the current {@link Place} of this knight piece.
     * All the returned {@link Move} should have source equal to the source parameter.
     * <p>
     * Hint: you should consider corner cases when the {@link Move} is not valid on the gameboard.
     * Several tests are provided and your implementation should pass them.
     * <p>
     * <strong>Attention: Student should make sure all {@link Move}s returned are valid.</strong>
     *
     * @param game   the game object
     * @param source the current place of the piece
     * @return an array of available moves
     */
    @Override
    public Move[] getAvailableMoves(Game game, Place source) {
        // All possible knight moves (dx, dy)
        int[][] knightMoves = {
            {2, 1}, {1, 2}, {-1, 2}, {-2, 1},
            {-2, -1}, {-1, -2}, {1, -2}, {2, -1}
        };

        List<Move> validMoves = new ArrayList<>();
        int size = game.getConfiguration().getSize();

        // Prepare rules for knight
        Rule[] rules = new Rule[] {
            new NilMoveRule(),
            new OutOfBoundaryRule(),
            new KnightMoveRule(),
            new KnightBlockRule(),
            new FirstNMovesProtectionRule(game.getConfiguration().getNumMovesProtection())
        };

        for (int[] move : knightMoves) {
            int destX = source.x() + move[0];
            int destY = source.y() + move[1];

            // Create destination Place and Move
            Place destination = new Place(destX, destY);
            Move candidateMove = new Move(source, destination);

            boolean isValid = true;
            for (Rule rule : rules) {
                if (!rule.validate(game, candidateMove)) {
                    isValid = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (isValid) {
                validMoves.add(candidateMove);
            }
        }

        return validMoves.toArray(new Move[0]);
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**
- The knight's possible moves are encoded as (dx, dy) pairs.
- For each possible move, we create a new `Place` and a `Move` from the current source.
- We check the move against all relevant rules: `NilMoveRule`, `OutOfBoundaryRule`, `KnightMoveRule`, `KnightBlockRule`, and `FirstNMovesProtectionRule`.
- Only moves that pass all rules are added to the result list.
- The result is returned as an array.

**Note:**  
- If your rules are managed elsewhere (e.g., by the game engine), you may need to adjust which rules you check here.
- This implementation assumes that the rules' `validate` methods are implemented and work as expected.

