Here is a complete implementation of the `JesonMor` class according to the requirements and the context of the JesonMor game (which is a chess-like game with knights):

```java
package assignment;

import assignment.piece.Knight;
import assignment.protocol.Color;
import assignment.protocol.Configuration;
import assignment.protocol.Game;
import assignment.protocol.Move;
import assignment.protocol.Piece;
import assignment.protocol.Place;
import assignment.protocol.Player;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * This class extends {@link Game}, implementing the game logic of JesonMor game.
 */
public class JesonMor extends Game {
    public JesonMor(Configuration configuration) {
        super(configuration);
    }

    @Override
    public Player start() {
        // reset all things
        Player winner = null;
        this.numMoves = 0;
        // Deep clone the initial board to avoid reference issues
        Piece[][] initial = configuration.getInitialBoard();
        int size = configuration.getSize();
        this.board = new Piece[size][size];
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < size; j++) {
                this.board[i][j] = initial[i][j];
            }
        }
        this.currentPlayer = null;
        this.refreshOutput();

        Player[] players = configuration.getPlayers();
        int playerCount = players.length;
        int turn = 0;

        while (true) {
            this.currentPlayer = players[turn % playerCount];
            Move[] availableMoves = getAvailableMoves(currentPlayer);
            if (availableMoves.length == 0) {
                // No available moves for current player, the other player wins
                winner = players[(turn + 1) % playerCount];
                break;
            }
            Move move = currentPlayer.nextMove(this, availableMoves);
            if (move == null) {
                // If player gives up or cannot move, the other player wins
                winner = players[(turn + 1) % playerCount];
                break;
            }
            Piece piece = getPiece(move.getSource());
            movePiece(move);
            numMoves++;
            updateScore(currentPlayer, piece, move);
            this.refreshOutput();
            winner = getWinner(currentPlayer, piece, move);
            if (winner != null) {
                break;
            }
            turn++;
        }

        if (winner != null) {
            System.out.println();
            System.out.println("Congratulations! ");
            System.out.printf("Winner: %s%s%s\n", winner.getColor(), winner.getName(), Color.DEFAULT);
            return winner;
        }
        return null;
    }

    @Override
    public Player getWinner(Player lastPlayer, Piece lastPiece, Move lastMove) {
        // Win by reaching the central place
        Place dest = lastMove.getDestination();
        Place central = getCentralPlace();
        if (dest.x() == central.x() && dest.y() == central.y()) {
            return lastPlayer;
        }

        // Win by capturing all opponent's pieces
        Player[] players = getPlayers();
        for (Player player : players) {
            if (!player.equals(lastPlayer)) {
                boolean hasPiece = false;
                for (int i = 0; i < board.length; i++) {
                    for (int j = 0; j < board[i].length; j++) {
                        Piece p = board[i][j];
                        if (p != null && p.getPlayer().equals(player)) {
                            hasPiece = true;
                            break;
                        }
                    }
                    if (hasPiece) break;
                }
                if (!hasPiece) {
                    return lastPlayer;
                }
            }
        }
        return null;
    }

    @Override
    public void updateScore(Player player, Piece piece, Move move) {
        Place src = move.getSource();
        Place dst = move.getDestination();
        int dist = Math.abs(src.x() - dst.x()) + Math.abs(src.y() - dst.y());
        player.setScore(player.getScore() + dist);
    }

    @Override
    public void movePiece(Move move) {
        Place src = move.getSource();
        Place dst = move.getDestination();
        Piece piece = board[src.x()][src.y()];
        board[src.x()][src.y()] = null;
        board[dst.x()][dst.y()] = piece;
    }

    @Override
    public Move[] getAvailableMoves(Player player) {
        List<Move> moves = new ArrayList<>();
        int size = board.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < size; j++) {
                Piece piece = board[i][j];
                if (piece != null && piece.getPlayer().equals(player)) {
                    Place source = new Place(i, j);
                    Move[] pieceMoves = piece.getAvailableMoves(this, source);
                    for (Move m : pieceMoves) {
                        // Validate move: destination must be in bounds and not occupied by own piece
                        Place dst = m.getDestination();
                        if (dst.x() >= 0 && dst.x() < size && dst.y() >= 0 && dst.y() < size) {
                            Piece destPiece = board[dst.x()][dst.y()];
                            if (destPiece == null || !destPiece.getPlayer().equals(player)) {
                                moves.add(m);
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return moves.toArray(new Move[0]);
    }
}
```

**Explanation of the implementation:**

- **start()**: Loops through players, gets their available moves, asks for their move, applies it, updates score, checks for winner, and continues until a winner is found.
- **getWinner()**: Checks if the last move landed on the central place (win condition), or if the opponent has no pieces left (win condition).
- **updateScore()**: Adds the Manhattan distance of the move to the player's score.
- **movePiece()**: Moves the piece from source to destination, removing it from the source.
- **getAvailableMoves()**: For each piece of the player, collects all valid moves (not out of bounds, not capturing own piece).

This implementation should work with the rest of your assignment code and pass the sample tests. If you have special rules for JesonMor, you can adjust the winner logic accordingly.

